From 2eb56b96274b1cee28cce55ae8844c0abf05c628 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <stew675@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 14:59:37 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 09/13] ggml-cuda : repack fp8 weights for the wmma kernel,
 ~204 -> ~186 us/launch

The block_f8_e4m3 rows are 132 B (f32 scale + 128 fp8), forcing 4-B
misaligned staging loads. The wmma kernel now reads a repacked layout
(fp8 bytes contiguous [m_pad][k] 16-B aligned + separate scales
[m_pad][n_col_blocks]) built once per tensor on first wmma use and cached
in the backend context (lazy, safe under CUDA graphs: the first call is
always during direct-execution warmup). Staging drops from 16 x uint to
4 x uint4 with no bounds predicates (pad rows pre-zeroed). GEMV/scalar
paths keep the original layout (decode untouched).

Measured (rig, gate 9216x512x2560): staging-stub delta 260.5 -> 175 us
(weight-side staging was ~33% of the kernel at 93 TFLOP/s); repacked
kernel 93 -> 106 TFLOP/s (+14%), bit-exact vs the block layout.
In-tree: wmma ~204 -> ~186 us/launch average, pp512 6220 -> ~6320
(+1.6-1.8%), tg64 unchanged, PPL 6.2677 exact, 47/47 GDN tests.

The repack kernel row loop must bound on m, not gridDim.y (m > 65535,
e.g. output.weight at vocab 152064, otherwise rows beyond 65535 are
never repacked - caught by an isolated device-vs-host repack check).

Assisted-by: DeepSeek V4
